{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 20, :comments ["<b>And he scattered it upon the surface of the water, and made the children of Israel drink it.</b> After it had become fine dust, he cast it upon the surface of the waters of the brook descending from the mountain, and he made them drink from those waters. And it is possible that the burnt gold, which was not smelted but rather burnt, and ground until fine, as R. Abraham Ibn Ezra mentioned, is light upon the surface of water; therefore it would float, and he made them drink it. Or he was scattering it upon the brook little by little, and drawing water and giving them to drink before it would sink. Or it was through a miraculous act. Now, he wished to disgrace their handiwork: he ground their god and introduced it into their bellies, so that they would expel it in their vomit and excrement, as it is stated (Isaiah 30:22), \"You will scatter them as an impure thing; you will say to it, 'Be gone!'\" And according to the opinion of our Sages (Avodah Zarah 44a), he further intended to test them in the manner of a sotah — and her belly shall swell and her thigh shall fall — and this is the truth."], :book :exodus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 32, :notes {0 "Ramban cites Isaiah 30:22; the word דָוָה (davah) literally means menstrual impurity, rendered here cryptically as 'impure thing.' The phrase כְּסוֹטוֹת refers to Numbers 5 the suspected adulteress (sotah), whose punishment causes her belly to swell (צָבְתָה) and thigh to fall (נָפְלָה)."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:20:03.215644498Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}